7592186129842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129169 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1045 "Type I and II Regions in Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129327 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c092012f-6280-5531-b39f-09c65d20ea5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186375288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129840 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129840 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f7/f7a556f986c44b922908b79e3982e098eb7250d9d470092ba2c45bbffe190913.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "771776cb-495a-5541-85ae-ed89a3b38002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129840 #edb/ref 1074 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=4.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/c5/c55d5f809462b767122ccc4af97c5b4d243730ed24f655bdc18e35ec57e25ce2.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=\\frac{y}{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1.$ So, we have $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460953 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460954 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460955 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7805c1fc-f8dc-58be-9c44-d22a8226c755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129842 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/24/24cbd9a0e01023dca6a289838d94efe3c7b67abe5b7a1f275f6945f280e65d64.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/6c/6c51abe5c21571a9c5596fce4e3b8ace210eacfab125016e6b9a3b2a7bc2e056.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/f9/f9fed5856eda128d84debec320bc38a35eaa2bf5184b1f81cb1194b02f8a306a.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1061 22589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b518252-fe1c-57d7-bc6f-6a00d3addac9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130023 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1061 22590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b6899de-a336-56a9-809a-31cc01aeebb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1061 22591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4858561d-380d-50d9-901d-e33ce219f2d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130025 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1061 22592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "952b663b-02bc-55c3-b391-c854df6de6fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130026 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1061 22593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eafe2c8b-e7b1-59e6-9468-2c731ee86e98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130027 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130028 #edb/ref 1061 22594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130028 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b78b691-c694-59d1-b1ef-5ea8a77ad679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130028 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}y=f(x)}$ and ${\\color{blue}y=g(x)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a45abf7904382a16daaa38ff617a9487b5c274cf65ef44f89e7feb0c32c496e0.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type I region**. Generally, a type I region is any region bounded by two vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b,$ and two curves $y=g(x)$ and $y=f(x),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions. \n\nUsing set notation, a type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type I region, any vertical line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7282efbe2c9244c77ce6bbbeca047ee3ab29dd2fc90e0d2440c519328cb5973.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type I region since there is a vertical line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a2/a26c7588e297a8af35f1e709210596a94f3fb4bd84e8237dc1462f48b961630e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1065 3854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2658cc5e-d169-5ff8-871c-4ccaab428eb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130371 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the region that lies between the curves ${\\color{red}x=f(y)}$ and ${\\color{blue}x=g(y)}$ over a closed interval $[a,b]$ on the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf2a2a41d63f21b279215fd1aa8d7361eb638e831b970bb3de861673f67612e6.png)\n\nThis region is an example of a **type II region**. In general, a type II region is any region that is bounded by two horizontal lines $y=a$ and $y=b,$ and two curves $x=g(y)$ and $x=f(y),$ where $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nUsing set notation, a type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn a type II region, any horizontal line drawn through the region should intersect the region in a *single* interval, as shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56019e8cc2b1146b99fa6f6532356281fdb847c342c25c63f843ddee4f8cb197.png)\n\nBy contrast, the following region is *not* a type II region since there is a horizontal line whose intersection with the region results in two *separate* line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once.\n\n![](images/a7/a7c1ead1274250f7184b132eff58a3028ce648715dcbfa7c7046077fd4e680ef.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1065 3855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d089f833-c09a-5229-9be0-8cffa2510d60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130372 #edb/ref 1067 "Type II Regions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, a given region can be type I, type II, both, or neither.\n\nFor example, let's consider the region in the coordinate plane bounded between the circle ${\\color{blue}x^2+y^2=2}$ and the lines ${\\color{red}x=-1},$ ${\\color{red}x=1},$ ${\\color{red}y=-1}$, and ${\\color{red}y=1},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db43502c2b096c912ac38d297c3a06e7dcbf4945af6fbc7f5e365a40d11fcde8.png)\n\nThere is a vertical line (for instance, the $y$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the vertical line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type I region.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1dca3b3015da15f554d95dae7add65aeee0b83a0075ec5295716a3e86d71f5.png)\n\nSimilarly, there is a horizontal line (for instance, the $x$-axis) whose intersection with the region results in two separate line segments. In other words, the horizontal line enters and leaves the region more than once. So, this region *cannot* be a type II region.\n\n![](images/82/82720f4b69fc3e63bbf54cb22d60299035faf91a6021f09d41e9f62dad571581.png)\n\nIn conclusion, this region is *neither* type I nor type II.\n\nLet's now look at an example that is *both* type I and type II." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1065 3856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "09369aae-8943-5475-9739-20e6619b16d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130373 #edb/ref 1067 "Regions that are Neither Type I Nor Type II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336117 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the listed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336117 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336117 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "099efdac-6222-5744-b3c1-1dad27d41b85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336484 #edb/ref 1080 "images/6c/6ccd325f24c9550f82a7063623db1cae0212a07f1c938b882c6707d35a6eee29.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336484 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336484 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f51b9b3-9edb-5ad5-a10c-52cfdfd9e751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336855 #edb/ref 1080 "images/5a/5a14be084ba22e33b636465156276fc489904a650077612c15fdec3634f8d902.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336855 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336855 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a4f7dba-2306-55b0-ad53-f7348ae38b3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337919 #edb/ref 1080 "images/5e/5e41509dcb689a5f16822eea9b8700171a74124789e12990ee4fd292d52aa2ea.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d8f500b5-47d1-5c54-82d7-03d7175ff4bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338160 #edb/ref 1080 "images/81/81b206d34bfa12b8623329616368ea3e6a978cf7963dbe5f09b23cafffb76dae.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f39c32b9-aa95-5261-a245-c6851bf03b8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186338160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186336484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338600 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b0492a08-2d29-5eca-af88-ac3ff00ea955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186514930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186515389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186515225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186341458 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2dd26fe5-df42-54f1-a759-c32dddc997da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342005 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342005 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c3/c316b62d5c1a08c6513413fd87c36c6feb26fb632709909a9c9f761d0806c4af.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80f56e91-1777-5d7a-b918-7d315f6b332a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342005 #edb/ref 1074 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-2(x-3)\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{y}{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $x=-\\sqrt{y}+2$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/fb/fb05eec73599c1beb8dd588c478208315f6edaf10af999115eb29fa10d5601e9.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=-\\sqrt{y}+2$ and leaves through the upper function $x=-\\frac{y}{2}+3.$ So, we have $-\\sqrt{y}+2\\le x\\le -\\frac{y}{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{y}+2\\le x\\le -\\frac{y}{2}+3\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468756 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468757 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468758 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the ellipses $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "451e02c3-10e2-5101-a83f-538a8be6b03d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342012 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/62/627e7e102a0f16cb0c4946e42b48ad122299d729c5bc419293e258a3f2e1701e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.25$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/d7/d7aa48c887d6fb1c3d0420867b7e74010fa93e16d7e24317e2cf214764e56113.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=0.5$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/ec/ec632fa5f86d8d9b34b9ccf661a3219e965f289c28bf69d7d80eeeb46093e7ae.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373175 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186373175 